1: # The actual characteristic pair of the selected-cusp source loop
2: 
3: Proved as P3K.42 after the full root audit and the independent
4: SP.2--4 frame/sign audit. We retain the exact positive rho
5: loop, initial physical-delta-edge fan, source, line and cap maps of
6: P3F.54, P3F.56 and P3K.40. The scalar is fixed and the coalescing finite
7: pair carries no logarithmic modification. The computation below uses
8: the actual normal framings on both caps of the F56 bordism. It does not
9: assign a value to another fan.
10: 
11: The resulting characteristic numbers in Jin's convention are
12: \[
13:                          (k,\ell)=(6,0).                 \tag{SP.1}
14: \]
15: Thus its first-resolution relative coordinates are
16: \((m,n)=(0,-6)\), with central coordinate one. The first fixed-edge
17: flop remains the value \(-5=23\) already proved in P3K.40.
18: 
19: ## SP.1. The framed local surgery and its actual four-cycle
20: 
21: Let \(e:S^3\hookrightarrow S^6\) be the actual lifted link of P3F.55.
22: Its projection is the immersion of complex Smale length two, and its
23: third normal field is the actual increasing height direction. Fix the
24: first normal field in the oriented complex normal plane of the
25: projection, and complete the frame \((\nu _1,\nu _2,\nu _3)\) with
26: that height field. Normalizing and applying Gram--Schmidt uses a
27: positive triangular homotopy. It therefore retains the actual
28: projection-normal plus height framing, rather than adding an element
29: of \(\pi _3(SO(3))\).
30: 
31: Takase, Corollary 2.15, supplies a Seifert embedding
32: \(V\hookrightarrow S^6\) whose outward boundary normal is this
33: specified \(\nu _1\). The proof of his Theorem 3.2 gives, for this
34: projection and this boundary normal,
35: \[
36:               2\omega=3\sigma(V)+e(\nu V)^2.             \tag{SP.2}
37: \]
38: The invariant and generator comparison in P3F.55, equations
39: (CL.16)--(CL.19), fixes the convention in (SP.2): the punctured
40: Kummer test has signature \(-16\) and Smale invariant \(-24\)
41: in both conventions. Hence the actual local projection has
42: \(\omega=-2\). The same comparison gives
43: \[
44:              8H(e)=e(\nu V)^2-\sigma(V),\qquad H(e)=1.
45:                                                                \tag{SP.3}
46: \]
47: Solving these two displayed equalities gives
48: \[
49:                     e(\nu V)^2=5,\qquad \sigma(V)=-3.    \tag{SP.4}
50: \]
51: 
52: We now identify these numbers with the characteristic numbers of the
53: *framed* surgery manifold, including its meridian line. Let
54: \[
55:  N_e=(S^6\setminus\operatorname{int}(S^3\times D^3))
56:                   \cup(D^4\times S^2)
57: \]
58: use precisely the preceding normal frame. Along the Seifert collar,
59: the vector from its boundary into \(V\) is \(-\nu _1\).
60: Consequently the truncated Seifert surface has boundary
61: \(S^3\times\{-\nu _1\}\), and caps by the literal constant section
62: \(D^4\times\{-\nu _1\}\). Denote this closed four-manifold by
63: \(\widehat V\).
64: 
65: Here are the orientations of this cap. Write the ambient orientation
66: near the knot as
67: \(\Omega_{S^3}\wedge dn_1\wedge dn_2\wedge dn_3\).
68: The Seifert orientation is
69: \(dn_1\wedge\Omega_{S^3}\), so its oriented normal two-plane is
70: \(-dn_2\wedge dn_3\). At \(-\nu _1\), the outward-first
71: orientation of the normal meridian sphere is also
72: \(-dn_2\wedge dn_3\). The boundary of the knot complement is
73: \(+\Omega_{S^3}\wedge\Omega_{S^2}\); the surgery cap therefore
74: has orientation \(-\Omega_{D^4}\wedge\Omega_{S^2}\).
75: The cap of \(\widehat V\) has orientation \(-\Omega_{D^4}\).
76: Thus its oriented normal plane is exactly the positive meridian
77: plane, with no sign change in its Euler class.
78: 
79: Let \(x\) be the meridian class with integral one on this sphere.
80: The Mayer--Vietoris map in degree two identifies the boundary
81: \(H_2(S^3\times S^2)=\mathbb Z\) with the meridian summands in
82: both pieces; it gives \(H^2(N_e)=\mathbb Zx\). In degree four,
83: the connecting map identifies \([\widehat V]\) with the oriented
84: boundary \([S^3]\). The normal-plane calculation above therefore
85: gives the actual duality identity
86: \[
87:        \operatorname{PD}_{N_e}(x)=[\widehat V],\qquad
88:        x|_{\widehat V}=e(\nu\widehat V).                 \tag{SP.5}
89: \]
90: The cap frame is constant. Its Euler square is the relative square
91: in (SP.4), and capping by a four-ball leaves the signature unchanged.
92: Restriction of the ambient tangent bundle to this embedded cycle is
93: \(T\widehat V\oplus\nu\widehat V\). It follows directly that
94: \[
95:  \begin{aligned}
96:  k(N_e)&=\int_{N_e}x^3=\int_{\widehat V}e(\nu\widehat V)^2=5,\\
97:  \ell(N_e)&=\int_{N_e}p_1(TN_e)x
98:        =3\sigma(\widehat V)+e(\nu\widehat V)^2=-4.
99:  \end{aligned}                                             \tag{SP.6}
100: \]
101: This constructs the four-cycle and its Euler-line restriction; it
102: does not recover the pair from the Haefliger number alone.
103: 
104: ## SP.2. The actual Cartier-to-Cox coordinate map
105: 
106: Use the normalization and branch notation of F56:
107: \[
108:  N=\mathbf P^1_s\times D_\rho,\quad
109:  x=s-\rho,\quad y=s+\rho,\quad Z=x/y,\quad
110:  D_+=(x=0),\quad D_-=(y=0).
111: \]
112: Remove the inner four-ball to obtain \(W\). The actual determinant
113: normal isomorphism of F56 is
114: \[
115:  L=\mathcal O(-D_+-D_-),\qquad
116:  E=\mathcal O(D_+)\oplus\mathcal O(-D_-),\qquad
117:  \operatorname{ad}E=\mathbb R_\gamma\oplus L_{\mathbb R}.
118:                                                                \tag{SP.7}
119: \]
120: The determinant trivialization is multiplication by \(x/y\).
121: In the finite \((s,\rho)\) ball, the Cartier frames of \(E\) are
122: \((1/x,y)\); their determinant is \(y/x\), so the stated
123: trivialization sends their wedge to one. These are nonvanishing
124: frames of the indicated line bundles even on the corresponding
125: divisors.
126: 
127: The morphism \(r=[x:y]:W\to\mathbf P^1\) identifies \(E\)
128: with \(r^*(\mathcal O(1)\oplus\mathcal O(-1))\). On the chart
129: \(y\ne0\), the coefficient arrow from the Cartier frames to the
130: standard dual-tautological and tautological frames is exactly
131: \[
132:                          \operatorname{diag}(1/y,y).      \tag{SP.8}
133: \]
134: Indeed the first pulled-back divisor frame is \(y/x\), and the
135: second divisor is absent from this chart. Equation (SP.8) follows by
136: expressing \(1/x\) and \(y\) in those frames. The corresponding
137: equation on \(x\ne0\) agrees on the overlap.
138: 
139: The actual Cox quaternion is
140: \((1+jZ)(c_0+j\overline{c_1})\). Its summand order is
141: \((c_0,\overline{c_1})=(\text{second},\text{first})\) in
142: (SP.7). After the positive Hermitian normalization of (SP.8), a
143: Cartier vector \((a,b)\) therefore becomes
144: \((\operatorname{phase}(y)b,\overline{\operatorname{phase}(y)}a)\)
145: in Cox order. Multiplication by the normalized first quaternion gives
146: \[
147:  g(x,y)=\frac1{\sqrt{|x|^2+|y|^2}}
148:        \begin{pmatrix}y&-\overline x\\x&\overline y\end{pmatrix}
149:                                                                \tag{SP.9}
150: \]
151: times the fixed summand swap. In particular (SP.9) is the
152: **Cartier-coordinate to Cox-coordinate** arrow. It is not its
153: inverse. A constant unitary adjustment of the swap makes it
154: determinant one and does not change its homotopy class.
155: 
156: The same fixed swap must also be kept on real normal coordinates.
157: Its adjoint action is
158: \[
159:                   (\gamma,\ell)\longmapsto
160:                            (-\gamma,\overline\ell).       \tag{SP.10}
161: \]
162: Both the real line and the complex plane reverse orientation;
163: their rank-three product preserves orientation. This is precisely
164: F56's constant positive-Hopf normal identification, retained at both
165: caps.
166: 
167: The inner projection-normal frame is the Cartier frame used here,
168: up to an extending gauge. To verify that assertion rather than
169: postulate it, use F56's explicit nonzero normal covector
170: \[
171:        (3s^2-\rho^2)\,dX-2s\,dy
172:                           +2\rho(s^2-\rho^2)\,d\rho .     \tag{SP.11}
173: \]
174: The determinant exact sequence identifies its normal quotient with
175: the canonical line of the normalization. The ratio of this line
176: identification and the Cartier identification is a holomorphic unit
177: on the punctured finite ball. Hartogs extension extends it to the
178: ball, and a holomorphic function in two variables cannot have its
179: only zero at the origin. Its extension is therefore a unit. The
180: resulting real two-plane frame comparison extends over the inner
181: four-ball. Adding the height line and the explicit triangular
182: graph-normal shear gives the actual frame of SP.1, with no free
183: \(SO(3)\) clutch.
184: 
185: The normal-coordinate unit can also be checked directly in both
186: actual fixed-edge charts. We use the physical source coordinate
187: \(q\) of F56, not its Tate multiple. With \(X=xy\), restriction
188: of the differential of that displayed source equation to the
189: normalization gives
190: \[
191:  dq=-\frac{xy}{xy+u(\rho)}\,n,
192:  \qquad n\text{ is the covector in (SP.11)}.              \tag{SP.11a}
193: \]
194: The first Cox chart has \(q=Zc_0c_1\), and the second has
195: \(q=Z^{-1}c'_0c'_1\), with \(c'_i=Zc_i\). Their quotient
196: complex normal coordinates consequently satisfy, on the fixed curve,
197: \[
198:  \begin{aligned}
199:  d(c_0c_1)&=-\frac{y^2}{xy+u(\rho)}\,n,\\
200:  d(c'_0c'_1)&=-\frac{x^2}{xy+u(\rho)}\,n.
201:  \end{aligned}                                             \tag{SP.11b}
202: \]
203: These are exactly the two Hom arrows of (SP.8), multiplied by the
204: same explicit unit \(-1/(xy+u(\rho))\). Since \(u(0)=u_*\ne0\),
205: it has an analytic logarithm and square root on the finite inner
206: ball. Its spinor adjustment therefore extends over that ball. This
207: verifies the actual normal-unit comparison, as well as its line
208: transition. It supplies no additional relative Chern number.
209: 
210: There is also an exact check on the separate normal factors. Once
211: their product has been matched by (SP.11b), any remaining
212: weight-preserving linear normal gauge has the form
213: \((c_0,c_1)\mapsto(g c_0,g^{-1}c_1)\). Remove its positive
214: modulus by the actual equivariant positive rescaling. For its unitary
215: phase, both right-complex spinor coordinates are multiplied by
216: \(g/|g|\); hence
217: \[
218:                   q_H\longmapsto q_H\,g/|g|.             \tag{SP.11c}
219: \]
220: This is the physical circle action, and its adjoint Hopf normal
221: coordinate is exactly unchanged. It contributes no additional
222: \(SO(3)\) clutch. A possible parameter winding of this circle-line
223: gauge is retained and extends over \(D^3\times S^1\), whose
224: restriction map on \(H^1\) is an isomorphism. We therefore do not
225: assume that each separate normal gauge has a logarithm across the
226: parameter origin.
227: 
228: ## SP.3. The outer cap: the actual product arrow
229: 
230: The outer coordinate change is the literal map of F56
231: \[
232:  A_\rho=\begin{pmatrix}1&-\rho\\1&\rho\end{pmatrix},
233:  \qquad
234:  \mathcal B_\rho(P)=
235:        \frac{A_\rho P A_\rho^*}{\operatorname{tr}(A_\rho P A_\rho^*)}.
236:                                                                \tag{SP.12}
237: \]
238: Here the closed three-ball is the positive semidefinite Hermitian
239: matrices of trace one. Its boundary consists of rank-one matrices.
240: This is a periodic diffeomorphism of the ball, including its centre,
241: and its boundary map is exactly \(r\). We use its actual product
242: extension on the surgery collar, rather than replacing the normal
243: frame by its derivative.
244: 
245: The collar in P3K.40 and F56 is the literal first-resolution product
